    "controlFlow": "Control flow refers to the order in which individual statements are executed. In nested if statements, control flow follows a depth-first pattern: the outer condition is checked first, and if true, control enters the outer block where the next condition is checked. This creates a sequential filtering effect — each level acts as a filter that only passes control deeper if its condition is satisfied.\n\nControl flow in a switch statement is based on jumping. The compiler evaluates the switch expression and jumps directly to the matching case label. This is different from if-else-if which evaluates each condition sequentially. For large numbers of cases, the compiler may use a jump table or hash lookup, making switch faster than if-else-if.",
