      "question": "Explain the concept of short-circuit evaluation in the context of nested if conditions. How does it affect performance?",
      "answer": "Short-circuit evaluation means that in logical operators && and ||, the second operand is evaluated only if necessary. In nested if, this applies to conditions at each level.\n\nFor &&: If the first condition is false, the second is not evaluated.\nFor ||: If the first condition is true, the second is not evaluated.\n\nIn nested if, short-circuit evaluation happens naturally:\nif (condition1) {        // If condition1 is false, we never reach here\n    if (condition2) {    // If condition2 is false, we never reach here\n        if (condition3) {\n            // All three conditions true\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nThis is more efficient than using && in a single condition when the conditions have side effects. Short-circuit evaluation improves performance by avoiding unnecessary computations and prevents errors like NullPointerException when checking nested object properties."
