      "answer": "In Java, each pair of curly braces {} creates a new block scope. Variables declared inside a block are only visible within that block and its nested sub-blocks.\n\nint a = 10;  // Outer scope\nif (a > 0) {\n    int b = 20;  // Outer if-block scope\n    if (a > 5) {\n        int c = 30;  // Inner if-block scope\n        System.out.println(a + b + c);  // OK\n    }\n    System.out.println(a + b);  // OK\n    // System.out.println(c);  // ERROR! c is out of scope\n}\n// System.out.println(b);  // ERROR! b is out of scope\n\nKey rules:\n- Outer variables are visible in inner blocks.\n- Inner variables are NOT visible in outer blocks.\n- Variables at the same level cannot see each other's blocks.\n- Different if blocks can have variables with the same name (separate scopes)."
